<h1>Roadmap</h1>
<p class="lede">
  What comes next, release by release. Each release prepares the one after it, so the order matters more than the
  dates, which are not fixed. What already shipped is in the <a href="<%= docs_changelog_path() %>">changelog</a>.
</p>

<h2 id="v0-3">0.3: a cluster you can operate</h2>
<p>
  Today a three-broker cluster replicates every partition, and a produce can go to any broker, but the membership is
  fixed at startup.
</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Leader routing</strong> (done). A produce sent to a follower is forwarded to the partition's leader, as
    the original caller, so clients can talk to any broker.</li>
  <li><strong>Safe membership changes.</strong> Add or remove one broker at a time without a restart, and the transport
    follows the current membership, so a removed broker can no longer connect.</li>
  <li><strong>Linearizable reads, as an opt-in.</strong> Read-index or lease reads, for consumers that must not see a
    stale replica.</li>
  <li><strong>Per-broker identity.</strong> mTLS with the broker id in the certificate, so one broker cannot act as
    another. Today brokers share one secret, which keeps outsiders out but lets any broker that holds it act as any
    member. With it: an idle timeout on Raft connections, and a handshake where the dialing broker proves itself
    first.</li>
  <li><strong>Topic deletion and consumer groups.</strong> A deleted topic is removed from the groups subscribed to
    it.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="v0-4">0.4: record format v2</h2>
<p>
  A record holds a key, a value and a timestamp, and segments store records as they arrived. Version 2 changes the
  format once, for everything that needs it.
</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Record headers</strong>, and attributes that mark a record as data or as a control record. Transactions
    write their commit and abort markers as control records.</li>
  <li><strong>Compression on disk</strong>, per batch. Gzip is only available on the binary protocol today.</li>
  <li><strong>Zero-copy consume.</strong> Once the batch on disk is the batch on the wire, the broker can send it from
    the page cache with <code>sendfile(2)</code>.</li>
  <li><strong>No migration step.</strong> Existing segments stay readable, and new segments are written in the new
    format.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="v0-5">0.5: object-store tiering</h2>
<p>
  Retention can already move old segments to a cold tier, but the only backend is a local directory, and nothing reads
  them back.
</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>An S3-compatible backend</strong> for the cold tier.</li>
  <li><strong>Reads that fall through to the cold tier</strong>, so a consumer can read offsets that left the local
    disk.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="v0-6">0.6: transactions across partitions</h2>
<p>
  Idempotent producers already make retries safe within a partition. Transactions make a set of writes across
  partitions, and the consumer offsets that led to them, visible all at once or not at all. This is the largest step,
  and it depends on 0.3 and 0.4.
</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Producer epochs and fencing.</strong> A producer that restarts gets a new epoch, and its old instance is
    shut out, so two copies of one producer cannot both write.</li>
  <li><strong>A transaction coordinator.</strong> It keeps each transaction's state in a replicated internal topic, so
    a transaction survives the loss of a broker.</li>
  <li><strong>Commit and abort markers</strong>, written into every partition the transaction touched, as control
    records.</li>
  <li><strong><code>read_committed</code> consumers.</strong> They stop at the last stable offset and skip the records
    of aborted transactions, using an index of those transactions.</li>
  <li><strong>Offsets inside a transaction</strong>, for exactly-once consume-transform-produce: read from one topic,
    write to another, and commit the read offset in the same transaction.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="not-planned">Not planned</h2>
<p>
  <code>es</code> stays one binary you can read in an afternoon. A ZooKeeper-style external coordinator, Kafka wire
  compatibility and a Windows build are out of scope.
</p>
